/-!
# Tits system, Weyl group, and Bruhat decomposition for Chevalley groups

For any Chevalley datum `D` and any field `K` (finite or infinite, of arbitrary characteristic),
the Borel-type subgroup `B = D.borelSubgroup K` and the Weyl subgroup `N = D.weylSubgroup K`
form a Tits system (BN-pair) on the extended elementary subgroup `D.extendedElementarySubgroup K`
generated by all root subgroups and the actual character-lattice torus
`T = (D.torusPoints K).range`.

We establish the full BN-pair package without restricting to infinite fields or simply connected
data:

* `ChevalleyDatum.borelSubgroup_inf_weylSubgroup`: `B ∩ N = T`, proved by showing first that
  elements of `B ∩ N` have trivial Weyl projection (using root inversions and negative-root
  disjointness from `B`), and then extending scalars to `AlgebraicClosure K` where the full-weight
  torus covers `T` and has trivial positive centralizer.
* `ChevalleyDatum.doubleCoset_weylElement_mul_doubleCoset_eq_of_mem_posRoots` and
  `ChevalleyDatum.doubleCoset_weylElement_mul_doubleCoset_subset`: sharp Bruhat double-coset
  multiplication `B nᵢ B w B = B nᵢ w B` when `w⁻¹(αᵢ) > 0` and `B nᵢ B w B ⊆ B nᵢ w B ∪ B w B`
  in general, using the punctured-positive factorization `U = U⁽ⁱ⁾ Xᵢ` and the rank-one exchange
  identity.
* `ChevalleyDatum.titsSystem`: the `TauCeti.TitsSystem` instance on
  `D.extendedElementarySubgroup K`.
* `ChevalleyDatum.titsSystemWeylGroupEquiv`, `ChevalleyDatum.ker_weylProjection`, and
  `ChevalleyDatum.weylQuotientEquiv`: `ker(weylProjection) = T` and `N / T ≃* W`, proved from the
  Tits length criterion `B s B w B = B s w B ↔ ℓ(w) < ℓ(s w)`.
* `ChevalleyDatum.doubleCoset_eq_iff_weylProjection_eq`,
  `ChevalleyDatum.disjoint_doubleCoset_iff_weylProjection_ne`, and
  `ChevalleyDatum.existsUnique_weyl_mem_doubleCoset`: the disjoint Bruhat decomposition of
  `D.extendedElementarySubgroup K` indexed by the Weyl group `W`.

## References

* R. Steinberg, *Lectures on Chevalley Groups*, Chapter 3, Lemma 22 and Theorem 4.
* R. W. Carter, *Simple Groups of Lie Type*, §§7.2 and 8.2–8.4.
* N. Bourbaki, *Lie Groups and Lie Algebras, Chapters 4–6*, Chapter IV, §2.
-/

public section

open scoped Pointwise
open Multiplicative
